WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.480
These two pieces are 8x10 Polacolor&nbsp;
by a contemporary photographer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:12.480
named Barbara Kasten. Now, Kasten constructs&nbsp;
abstract room-sized interior environments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:20.480
and she's using complex lighting and colored&nbsp;
gels, geometric shapes made of plaster,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:28.880
all kinds of objects: columns and mirrors,&nbsp;
translucent colored scrims. She takes advantage of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:35.440
shadows and fiberglass mesh, all of this kind&nbsp;
of stuff that you get at a hardware store, or an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:42.240
architectural construction site I think. So, these&nbsp;
constructions are really enormous stage sets that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:49.280
exemplify her material, physical, and theatrical&nbsp;
interests. So she will use all of these pieces,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:54.080
put them on the big stage, and then she starts&nbsp;
to think about the materiality of the sculpture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.720 --> 00:01:03.280
their painterly ideas, and she would create these&nbsp;
non-narrative abstract geometric compositions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:09.040
And they were really really stunning, and I think&nbsp;
you may get an idea of how much of a magician she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:14.880
was with animating the quality of light. And&nbsp;
what she did is she would set the stage with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:20.000
her objects, all of which were white, except of&nbsp;
course for the mirrors and glass and hooks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.720 --> 00:01:27.120
wires and things like that. She would look&nbsp;
through the camera viewfinder again and again,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:34.240
moving objects, moving light until she saw&nbsp;
what she thought she wanted as her photograph&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:41.760
in the viewfinder. So, with each object that&nbsp;
was on the set she had a strobe and that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:48.800
tested with a colored gel to see how the light&nbsp;
would cover the object that she was targeting.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:56.720
The camera's aperture was set to f64 to provide&nbsp;
this deep space depth of field in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:04.160
secure really sharp edge focus for these sets.&nbsp;
Then, all the studio lights were turned off,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:12.080
each strobe then was individually flashed. Once&nbsp;
each of these objects was painted with light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:18.640
the result became these breathtaking abstract&nbsp;
scenes in these luminous lush colors.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.520 --> 00:02:25.200
These are from a whole body of work that she&nbsp;
did called "Constructs." So she was really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:31.200
falling back on her interests as a painter, in&nbsp;
sculpture, and theater, and these I think are just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:49.840
gorgeous iridescent photographs that exemplify&nbsp;
this kind of fabrication to be photographed.

